What "adopt ExoPlayer" means here, concretely
---------------------------------------------
"ExoPlayer" today lives under the AndroidX **Media3** umbrella
(`androidx.media3`). Adopting it means adding the `media3-exoplayer` playback
engine and, for the media card/notification, `media3-session` (whose
`MediaSessionService` is the modern replacement for the hand-rolled framework
`MediaSession` + foreground `Service` we currently run in
`WcastMediaService.kt`). It also pulls in `media3-common` and
`media3-datasource`. There is no Compose impact: `media3-ui` (a View-based
player widget) would NOT be used.

There are two distinct "levels" of adoption, with very different cost/benefit:

- Level A — drop-in swap: keep the existing per-utterance WAV pipeline and
  look-ahead (ticket 02) in `SpeechController`, but replace the framework
  `MediaPlayer` with ExoPlayer and replace `WcastMediaService`'s hand-rolled
  session/notification with `MediaSessionService`. Same behavior, cleaner
  stack, better media card.
- Level B — continuous timeline: stop treating each paragraph as a separate
  file and instead expose the whole article as one seekable timeline (a growing
  playlist / `ConcatenatingMediaSource`). This unlocks a *real* seek bar and
  continuous position; it is a genuine rework of `SpeechController`'s state
  machine.

The media card (notification + lock screen) — what changes
----------------------------------------------------------
Today (`WcastMediaService.notify`):
- `Notification.MediaStyle` with a framework `MediaSession`, four actions
  (previous / play-pause / next / **stop**), and `PlaybackState` published with
  `PLAYBACK_POSITION_UNKNOWN` — so there is **no seek bar and no duration** on
  the lock screen or in the drawer.

With `media3-session` + `MediaSessionService`:
- The notification is generated for you (media3's
  `DefaultMediaNotificationProvider`, built on `androidx.media`). You get, for
  free: a real **seek bar**, elapsed/total time, artwork slot, and the standard
  transport buttons — the "podcast-like" card ticket 09 flagged.
- It keeps working across lock screen, notification shade, Android Auto,
  Wear OS, and Bluetooth (AVRCP) surfaces, driven by the same session.
- Differences to expect:
  * The default layout does **not** show an explicit "Stop" button — only
    play/pause + skip (and rewind/forward when available). To keep Stop I'd add
    a custom `MediaNotification.Provider`.
  * Our hand-written "Now playing"/"Paused" text and the exact icon/compact-view
    arrangement are replaced by media3's defaults (still customizable, but it's
    new code to own).
  * Title/artist come from `MediaItem.MediaMetadata`, so we'd set the article
    title there instead of `setContentTitle`.

Seeking, playing, and tracking location — current vs. ExoPlayer
---------------------------------------------------------------
Current behavior:
- **Playing**: `SpeechController.playFile` plays one WAV per utterance through a
  framework `MediaPlayer`, chained by `onCompletionListener` →
  `advanceAfterUtterance`, with the ticket-02 look-ahead rendering the next
  chunk while the current one plays.
- **Seeking**: there is *no* in-paragraph seek and *no* seek bar. "Skip
  forward/back" (`skipSections(delta)` → `enqueueFrom`) re-renders from a
  retained *paragraph index*; it jumps at section boundaries, not to an
  arbitrary time.
- **Tracking location**: position is tracked at the *paragraph* level, not
  continuously. `onUtteranceStarted` fires when each utterance begins, and
  `MainViewModel` turns that into `ActivePosition(sectionId, paragraphIndex)`,
  which drives the left-margin highlight, the "jump to current position"
  affordance, and the saved bookmark resume point (`speakFromParagraph`). There
  is no notion of "23 seconds into this paragraph."

With ExoPlayer:
- **Playing**: ExoPlayer plays the same rendered WAVs natively (it has a WAV/PCM
  extractor). The look-ahead is preserved by appending the next `MediaItem` to
  the playlist the moment it finishes rendering; `onMediaItemTransition` takes
  over the role of `onCompletionListener`.
- **Seeking**: `player.seekTo()` gives true, sample-accurate seeking — but only
  *within already-rendered audio*. To seek "to 20:00" across the article, all
  audio up to 20:00 must already exist. With the current lazy look-ahead
  rendering, a forward seek past the next chunk still has to synthesize on
  demand (same as today). A genuinely useful cross-article seek bar therefore
  requires Level B and, practically, pre-rendered audio (ticket 21) — which is
  exactly a continuous `.m4a`.
- **Tracking location**: `Player.Listener` reports continuous position; you map
  position → `(sectionId, paragraphIndex)` through a per-item duration/cue
  table and keep driving the same highlight/scroll callback. Strictly more
  precise than today's boundary-only tracking, and it's what a real seek bar
  needs.

The key architectural tension (worth naming explicitly)
-------------------------------------------------------
Today's pipeline is *lazy*: audio exists only for the current + one look-ahead
chunk. A seek bar and continuous position are naturally features of a
*complete, seekable* source. ExoPlayer can't invent audio that hasn't been
synthesized yet. So the honest options are:
1. Keep lazy rendering and adopt ExoPlayer only at Level A — you get the nicer
   media card, but the seek bar is effectively cosmetic (seek within the
   current chunk only).
2. Adopt Level B and accept that meaningful seeking needs the article
   pre-rendered (ticket 21) — which is where ExoPlayer's real value is.

Risks of breaking existing functionality
----------------------------------------
1. **Headset/Bluetooth media-key routing (tickets 07/09)** — the hardest-won
   behavior. Today it is hand-rolled: `setMediaButtonReceiver` +
   `MediaButtonReceiver` + `FLAG_HANDLES_MEDIA_BUTTONS`. Media3 routes buttons
   through `MediaSessionService` + `onMediaButtonEvent` instead. This is the
   single highest regression risk; it must be re-tested explicitly with wired
   and Bluetooth buttons after adoption.
2. **Audio focus** — today hand-managed (`requestAudioFocus`/`abandon`: stop on
   LOSS, pause + auto-resume on TRANSIENT, duck ignored). Media3 owns focus
   automatically; our `pausedByFocusLoss` auto-resume logic needs to be mapped
   onto Media3's focus handling, and phone-call behavior re-verified.
3. **The per-chunk state machine** — `generation`, `currentIndex`,
   `synthInFlight`, `readyToPlayIndex`, and the stale-callback guards all hang
   off `MediaPlayer` callbacks. ExoPlayer swaps `onCompletionListener` for
   `Player.Listener` (`onMediaItemTransition`/`onPlaybackStateChanged`), so this
   is the largest code change and where subtle bugs (e.g. the gap-closing
   look-ahead) could regress.
4. **Pause/resume mid-synthesis** — the "cancel synthesis and re-render on
   resume" path survives (it is on the TTS side, not the player side), but the
   `player != null` vs `synthInFlight` branching must be re-expressed in
   ExoPlayer's terms (is an item loaded, or still rendering?).
5. **Notification shape** — losing the explicit Stop action and the exact
   compact-view arrangement is user-visible (cosmetic, but you will notice it).
6. **Dependency/API surface** — media3 is stable and widely used; minSdk 26 and
   targetSdk 34 are fine; no conflict with Compose (we would not use media3-ui)
   or with Kotlin 1.9.24 / AGP 8.5.2. `media3-session` adds `androidx.media`
   plus a small futures library transitively. Size (~2–4 MB) you have already
   said is fine.

Recommendation
--------------
Sequence it — do not swap everything at once:

1. **Now (low risk, immediate win):** adopt `media3-session` only —
   `MediaSessionService` + `MediaController` — replacing `WcastMediaService`'s
   hand-rolled `MediaSession`/notification, while `SpeechController` keeps its
   per-chunk framework `MediaPlayer` and look-ahead. This gives the modern
   media card (seek bar + duration + position) and deletes a lot of hand-rolled
   plumbing, with the smallest blast radius. Position would be published
   manually from the framework `MediaPlayer` until the engine swap.
2. **When ticket 21 lands:** swap the playback engine to `media3-exoplayer` and
   point it at the pre-rendered `.m4a` + cue table. That is the natural,
   continuous, seekable source ExoPlayer wants — real paragraph seek + real
   position tracking — without touching the live per-chunk pipeline at all.
3. **Only if you later want a live seek bar:** do the Level B rework of the
   lazy pipeline. Defer indefinitely; steps 1 + 2 cover the podcast feel you
   are after.

Net: nothing about "adopt ExoPlayer now" has to break current playback — the
risk is concentrated in the media-button/focus/notification plumbing, and it
can be isolated by doing the session layer first.

